var i=[{slug:`six-step-storage-pipeline`,title:`The six-step pipeline hiding in every backend`,description:`Load, validate, transform, encrypt, serialize, version. The same storage chores keep showing up in unrelated projects.`,lede:`Every project I built needed to store a single object. A config file, a local state file, a preferences blob. The object changed. The storage code did not.`,topic:`Persistence`,tags:[`.NET`,`Persistence`,`Architecture`],relatedCaseStudy:{slug:`singular-framework-core`,name:`Singular Framework Core`},sections:[{heading:`One object, six chores`,paragraphs:[`The object was never the hard part. The hard part was everything around it: read the file, check that the bytes still make sense, transform the model into the stored shape, encrypt it if it needs encryption, serialize it, write it somewhere, and deal with the day the shape changes.`,`That is the pipeline. It is not the product. It is the work you do before the product starts.`,`I kept rewriting it. Each project had its own version, its own edge cases, and its own small mistakes. The code was never the reason the project existed.`],bullets:[`Load: read the stored value from a file, a cloud store, or memory.`,`Validate: reject corrupt or incompatible input before it reaches the rest of the app.`,`Transform: reshape the model into the form the storage layer expects.`,`Encrypt: protect the value at rest when the data requires it.`,`Serialize: turn the object into bytes or text.`,`Version and migrate: keep old files readable when the model changes.`]},{heading:`The version step is the one people skip`,paragraphs:[`Most of the steps are easy to see. The version step is the one that waits until later. The class changes, the file on disk does not, and suddenly the app has to understand two shapes at once.`,`A flat migration list makes this worse. You end up registering every pair of versions you might jump between. Add a new version and the number of combinations grows again. Miss one and the failure shows up at runtime, often in production.`,`A graph fixes the shape of the problem. Register each migration once as an edge, then find the path from the version on disk to the version in memory. I wrote about that separately, because it deserves its own article.`]},{heading:`What I changed`,paragraphs:[`Singular Framework Core is built around the pipeline instead of around one storage backend. The core library defines the interfaces. Storage, serialization, and processing are separate packages, so a consumer installs only the pieces it calls.`,`The pipeline has four phases: pre-processing, serialization, post-processing, and storage. Encryption is a processor. Reshaping bytes is a processor. Changing properties before storage is a processor. The pipeline does not need to know what any of them do.`],code:{language:`csharp`,code:`public class AppConfig
{
    public string ApiKey { get; set; }
    public int TimeoutSeconds { get; set; }
}

var singular = new Singular<AppConfig, string>(
    serializer: new NewtonsoftJsonSerializer<AppConfig>(),
    storage: new FileSystemTextRepository("config.json"),
    processors: new IDataProcessorLayer<AppConfig, string>[]
    {
        new SymmetricEncryptorLayer<AppConfig, string>()
    }
);

await singular.CreateAsync(new AppConfig { ApiKey = "...", TimeoutSeconds = 30 });
var config = await singular.ReadAsync();
await singular.UpdateAsync(new AppConfig { ApiKey = "...", TimeoutSeconds = 60 });
await singular.DeleteAsync();`}},{heading:`What it costs`,paragraphs:[`A package family is not free. Five packages means five version numbers, five changelogs, and a compatibility matrix. I underestimated that part. A single library would have been simpler to publish.`,`The trade-off is that a single library forces every consumer to carry code they never call. If the variants are real, the coordination cost is worth it. If they are hypothetical, it is not.`]},{heading:`When not to use it`,paragraphs:[`If you have one file and no versioning, write the file. If you have relational data, use Entity Framework Core. If the storage shape never changes, the abstraction is overhead.`,`The pipeline is worth building when the same storage chores repeat across projects, and when the storage backend or serialization format is genuinely allowed to vary.`]}],takeaways:[`The storage pipeline is usually more reusable than the feature that triggered it.`,`Versioning belongs in the storage design, not in a cleanup pass later.`,`Composability is worth package coordination only when the variants are real.`]},{slug:`graph-based-migrations`,title:`A graph is the right shape for migrations`,description:`Flat migration lists grow quadratically. Edges and path finding turn version history into one registration per step.`,lede:`A UserV1 becomes a UserV2, which becomes a UserV3. Most migration systems make you register every pair. A graph lets you register each step once.`,topic:`Data modeling`,tags:[`C#`,`.NET`,`Data modeling`],relatedCaseStudy:{slug:`entity-migration-framework`,name:`Entity Migration Framework`},sections:[{heading:`The pairwise trap`,paragraphs:[`Migrating version 3 to version 7 should not require a version 3 to version 7 method. A flat migration list pushes you toward pairwise registrations: v3 to v4, v4 to v5, v5 to v6, v6 to v7, and then every other combination someone might need.`,`It works until it does not. The list grows with every version, and the failure mode is quiet. A missing pair is found when someone asks for it, often in production.`]},{heading:`Register edges, not routes`,paragraphs:[`Each migration is an edge between two versions. The registry is a directed graph. When you ask for a migration from the version on disk to the version in memory, the framework finds a path and composes the steps.`,`A new version registers one edge, not every pairwise route to every older version. If no path exists, registration can fail loudly before the code ships.`]},{heading:`Type safety is part of the design`,paragraphs:[`A migration takes one type and returns another. Without generics, the return type is object and every consumer has to cast. The compiler cannot help with the part that changes most.`,`The generic version makes the source and target part of the migration type. The migrate method returns the target directly, so there is no cast and no runtime type error caused by a migration mismatch.`],code:{language:`csharp`,code:`public class UserV1ToV2 : IMigration<UserV1, UserV2>
{
    public UserV2 Migrate(UserV1 src) => new()
    {
        FullName = $"{src.FirstName} {src.LastName}"
    };
}

var manager = new MigrationBuilder<User>()
    .AddMigration<UserV1ToV2, UserV1, UserV2>()
    .Build();

var v2User = manager.Migrate<UserV1, UserV2>(v1User);
var v3User = manager.Migrate<UserV1, UserV3>(v1User); // composed automatically`}},{heading:`The cost`,paragraphs:[`A graph has more machinery than a flat list. For a one-time migration, or a schema with fewer than three versions, the flat list is fine. The graph earns its complexity when versions evolve often or when many versions are reachable from one another.`,`The other cost is setup. The library asks you to build a registry, which is flexible but adds friction. A sensible default registry would make the first run smoother.`]},{heading:`What I would do first`,paragraphs:[`I spent more time debugging edge cases in the traversal than I should have. Writing the path resolution algorithm down before implementing it would have surfaced the awkward cases earlier.`,`For a library like this, the documentation is half the product. The code can be correct and still fail to land if the first example is hard to follow.`]}],takeaways:[`Register each migration once as an edge, not every pair as a route.`,`A missing path should fail at registration, not at runtime.`,`Use the graph when versions evolve often. Use the flat list when they do not.`]},{slug:`client-side-scores-are-not-validation`,title:`Client-side scores are not validation`,description:`A browser can lie. Moving the trust boundary from the UI to the server made every score verifiable.`,lede:`The academy typing tool calculated scores in the browser. Anyone with DevTools could change the number and submit it. The fix was not a better lock. It was moving the trust boundary.`,topic:`Trust boundaries`,tags:[`Security`,`Blazor`,`.NET`],relatedCaseStudy:{slug:`academic-typing-training`,name:`ATT (Academic Typing Training)`},sections:[{heading:`The exploit was one edit`,paragraphs:[`The previous tool calculated the score in JavaScript and submitted the final number. A student could open DevTools, change the number, and submit. The teacher had no way to know whether a score was real.`,`The obvious fix is to make cheating harder. Block right-click, disable DevTools, minify the JavaScript. Each of those is a deterrent. None of them changes who is allowed to decide the score.`]},{heading:`Deterrents are not a trust boundary`,paragraphs:[`Anything that runs in the browser can be changed by the person using the browser. Obfuscation raises the effort. It does not create trust. The browser is still the place where the number is produced.`,`The useful question is not "how do I stop them from editing this?" It is "why is the browser deciding this at all?"`]},{heading:`Re-grade the raw input`,paragraphs:[`The rebuilt platform moved grading to the server. The browser submits the raw input: the typed text, the timing, and the target paragraph. The server computes the score. The client never sends a number.`,`Forging a score now means forging a complete typing record that matches the target text. At that point, the cheapest way to fake the result is to type the text.`,`The teacher could trust the score because the authority moved. The UI did not need to become unhackable. It needed to stop being the judge.`]},{heading:`The same pattern outside typing`,paragraphs:[`This is not a typing problem. Any score, price, permission, or eligibility check that the browser decides can be changed in the browser. If the value matters, the server should own the calculation and the client should send inputs.`,`The pattern is the same in commerce, assessments, access control, and anywhere the UI is treated as a security boundary. The UI is a presentation layer. It is not an authority.`]},{heading:`The teacher was the real user`,paragraphs:[`The obvious build was for students: more practice modes, better keyboard animations, a nicer typing view. But the person drowning in work was the teacher. She distributed every paragraph to every student by hand and reviewed every submission manually.`,`The platform turned distribution into one action: one assignment, one class, one push. That change mattered more than any student-facing feature, because it removed the work that made the tool expensive to run.`]}],takeaways:[`If the browser decides a value that matters, the browser can lie.`,`Deterrents raise effort. They do not create a trust boundary.`,`Move the calculation to the server and accept raw inputs, not final scores.`]}];function o(e){return i.find(r=>r.slug===e)??null}function s(e){let a=[e.title,e.description,e.lede,...e.tags,...e.takeaways,...e.sections.flatMap(t=>[t.heading,...t.paragraphs,...t.bullets??[]])].join(` `).trim().split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean).length;return Math.max(1,Math.round(a/220))}function n(e){return{meta:`Essay \xB7 ${s(e)} min read`,title:e.title,description:e.description,tags:e.tags.join(` · `),href:`/writing/${e.slug}`}}function h(e){return{title:`${e.title} \xB7 Mohammad Ayaad`,description:e.description,ogDescription:e.lede,path:`/writing/${e.slug}`,ogType:`article`}}export{s as a,o as i,i as n,n as r,h as t};